<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Former Qin</h1><p class="st-meta">351 CE – 394 CE · capital Chang'an</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Di-led empire of Fu Jian, which unified northern China for the first time since 316, then collapsed after its defeat at the Fei River in 383.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fu-hong">Fu Hong</a>, a Di chieftain of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a>, served <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-zhao">Later Zhao</a> and moved west when it collapsed. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fu-jian-337-385">Fu Jian</a> (the elder) took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> in 351 and proclaimed the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>. In 357 his nephew Fu Jian overthrew the tyrannical emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fu-sheng">Fu Sheng</a> and took the title Heavenly King.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fu-jian-337-385">Fu Jian</a> and his chief minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-meng-former-qin">Wang Meng</a> built an effective Chinese-style administration, promoted Confucian learning and agriculture and treated defeated peoples generously. They conquered <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/former-yan">Former Yan</a> in 370, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chouchi">Chouchi</a> in 371, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/former-liang">Former Liang</a> and Dai in 376 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-river">Han River</a> fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiangyang">Xiangyang</a> in 379, uniting northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and reaching into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fu-jian-337-385">Fu Jian</a>'s invasion of the south ended in rout at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fei-river">Fei River</a> in 383 against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/eastern-jin">Eastern Jin</a>. Former subjects rebelled, founding <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-yan">Later Yan</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-qin">Later Qin</a>, and in 385 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yao-chang">Yao Chang</a> captured and killed Fu Jian. Remnants under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fu-deng">Fu Deng</a> and Fu Chong lingered until 394.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/former-qin.jpg" alt="Former Qin" width="320" height="227"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sixteen_Kingdoms_369_AD.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>351 CE – 394 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chang'an</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Fu Jian, Fu Deng</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Chinese ancestral religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Qin (Fu Jian)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=372&amp;focus=former-qin" aria-label="Former Qin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7449 935 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Former Qin lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7769 1191L7867 1006M7769 1191L7616 1132M7769 1191L8033 1602M7769 1191L7769 1191M7769 1191L7743 987M7769 1191L7769 1191M7769 1191L7706 1209"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7867 1006h0M7616 1132h0M8033 1602h0M7769 1191h0M7743 987h0M7769 1191h0M7706 1209h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7769 1191h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7769 1191h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Chang'an. <a href="/atlas?year=372&amp;focus=former-qin">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

